-- Lesson enrichment, November 9, 2026: English 1, English 2 and United States History 1.
-- Adds a "Worked example" and "Common mistake" paragraph before "Your task:" in each of the 72 lessons.
-- Safe to run more than once (skips lessons that already contain "Worked example:").
-- Each statement keys on course slug and lesson position.

-- ENGLISH 1
with e(pos, txt) as (values
(1, $e$Worked example: in "The Gift of the Magi," Della wants to buy Jim a worthy Christmas gift, but she has only one dollar and eighty seven cents. That is what stands in the way. She sells her hair to buy a chain for his watch, and the turning point comes when Jim reveals that he sold the watch to buy combs for her hair. The conflict is external, because money is the obstacle, and also internal, because each must give up something precious. Common mistake: do not retell every event. Name the want, the obstacle and the turning point, and your answer stays short and strong.$e$),
(2, $e$Worked example: when Della sells her hair, the narrator does not stop to say she loves Jim. Her action shows it. That is indirect characterization. When the narrator calls the couple wise, that is direct characterization. A strong answer pairs a trait with proof, such as "Della is generous, because she sells her most prized possession for her husband." Common mistake: naming a trait without evidence. Always use the word because, then point to what the character says, does or thinks.$e$),
(3, $e$Worked example: in Genesis 22, Abraham rises up early in the morning, saddles his donkey, and travels for three days to the mountain. The setting is a lonely mountain in the land of Moriah, in ancient times. The mood is tense and solemn. Verse 7 builds it with Isaac's question: "Behold the fire and the wood: but where is the lamb for a burnt offering?" The quiet walk and that simple question create the feeling. Common mistake: confusing setting with mood. Setting is where and when. Mood is what the reader feels.$e$),
(4, $e$Worked example: a theme is a message about life, stated as a full sentence, not a single word. "Forgiveness" is a topic. "A loving father welcomes home the one who repents" is a theme. In Luke 15:20 the father, while the son was yet a great way off, saw him, had compassion and ran to him. That detail proves the theme. The story is told in third person, because the narrator speaks of he and they and not I. Common mistake: stopping at one word. Turn your theme into a sentence with a subject and a verb.$e$),
(5, $e$Worked example: Robert Frost's poem begins, "Two roads diverged in a yellow wood." The speaker is a traveler who must choose. The subject is a decision. The poem has four stanzas of five lines each. Psalm 1 also presents a choice, between the way of the righteous and the way of the ungodly, but the psalm states which way is blessed, while Frost leaves the traveler to wonder. Common mistake: calling the speaker the poet. The speaker is the voice in the poem, who may differ from the author.$e$),
(6, $e$Worked example: Psalm 19:1 says, "The heavens declare the glory of God; and the firmament sheweth his handywork." The image of the sky appeals to sight. Verse 2 adds a sound device called parallelism: "Day unto day uttereth speech, and night unto night sheweth knowledge." Two lines repeat the same pattern to say the same thing in two ways. Verse 5 uses a simile, comparing the sun to a bridegroom coming out of his chamber. Common mistake: listing an image without naming the sense. Always say which sense it reaches: sight, sound, touch, taste or smell.$e$),
(7, $e$Worked example: "The LORD is my shepherd" is a metaphor, because it says one thing is another without using like or as. It means that God guides, feeds and protects His people the way a shepherd cares for sheep. A simile would say, "The LORD is like a shepherd." A symbol is an object that stands for an idea. The rod and the staff stand for protection and guidance. Common mistake: explaining a metaphor word for word. Ask what the comparison tells you about God, not only what the shepherd does.$e$),
(8, $e$Worked example: notice the shift in Psalm 23. In verses 1 to 3 the speaker says "he" and talks about God. In verse 4 the speaker turns and says "thou art with me," speaking directly to God in the darkest valley. That change in address is a poetic device, and it makes the comfort feel close and personal. A strong paragraph names the device, quotes the phrase, then explains the effect. Common mistake: only saying that the poem is comforting. Show how the words produce the comfort.$e$),
(9, $e$Worked example: the Gettysburg Address begins in the past, "Four score and seven years ago," moves to the present with "Now we are engaged in a great civil war," and ends with the future task, "It is for us the living." The main structure is chronological, or sequence. The signal words now and ago show time. A second structure also appears, because the speech names a problem, a divided nation, and calls for a solution. Common mistake: choosing a structure without proof. Always list the signal words that convinced you.$e$),
(10, $e$Worked example: Ecclesiastes 3:1 says, "To every thing there is a season, and a time to every purpose under the heaven." The central idea is that life moves through appointed seasons, each with its own purpose. The details, such as a time to be born and a time to die, a time to weep and a time to laugh, support that idea. Lincoln's phrase "With malice toward none, with charity for all" has a different central idea, which is healing the nation after war. Common mistake: copying one detail and calling it the central idea. The central idea covers every detail.$e$),
(11, $e$Worked example: Douglass speaks to a gathering that celebrates freedom, yet he says, "This Fourth of July is yours, not mine." His purpose is to persuade the audience to see the contradiction between their celebration and slavery. His perspective is that of a man who was once enslaved, so his experience gives him authority. Evidence for purpose is his direct address to the audience. Evidence for perspective is the contrast between "yours" and "mine." Common mistake: mixing purpose and perspective. Purpose is why the author writes. Perspective is where the author stands.$e$),
(12, $e$Worked example: a summary is shorter than the original and keeps only the main points in your own words, with no opinion. A paraphrase restates a passage in about the same length. Take Proverbs 15:1: "A soft answer turneth away wrath: but grievous words stir up anger." A paraphrase might read, "A gentle reply calms anger, but harsh words make it grow." Common mistake: swapping a few words and keeping the original structure. Change the sentence pattern as well, and give credit to the author.$e$),
(13, $e$Worked example: here is a short argument. Claim: students should read for fifteen minutes every day. Reason: daily reading builds vocabulary. Evidence: readers meet new words in context again and again, and repetition helps words stay in memory. Counterclaim: some students have no free time. Rebuttal: fifteen minutes can fit into a bus ride or a break, and the gain lasts all year. Common mistake: stating an opinion without a reason. Remember that a claim must be supported.$e$),
(14, $e$Worked example: compare two claims. Weak claim: "Sleep is important." Anyone would agree, so there is nothing to prove. Strong claim: "Students should protect eight to ten hours of sleep on school nights, because rest improves memory and attention." Teens are commonly advised to sleep eight to ten hours, so this claim can be supported with evidence from health organizations. Common mistake: using a claim too broad to prove. A good claim is specific, debatable and supported by two clear reasons.$e$),
(15, $e$Worked example: Paul opens in Acts 26:2 with "I think myself happy, king Agrippa, because I shall answer for myself this day before thee." That respectful tone builds ethos, or credibility, since he shows he is courteous and knows the king's expertise. In verse 8 he reasons, "Why should it be thought a thing incredible with you, that God should raise the dead?" That question is logos, an appeal to logic. When he describes his own change of heart, he stirs feeling, which is pathos. Common mistake: naming the appeal without a quotation. Always quote a few words and explain.$e$),
(16, $e$Worked example: Proverbs 15:22 says, "Without counsel purposes are disappointed: but in the multitude of counsellors they are established." The claim is that plans succeed when we seek wise advice. A life example is a student who asks a teacher before starting a big project and avoids mistakes. The strongest appeal is ethos, because the proverb carries the authority of tested wisdom. Common mistake: choosing an example that does not fit the claim. Check that your example proves the exact point of the verse.$e$),
(17, $e$Worked example: a weak thesis says, "I will write about reading." A strong thesis takes a position: "Daily reading strengthens vocabulary, builds focus and prepares students for lifelong learning." That thesis suggests a three point outline: first vocabulary, second focus, third lifelong learning. Under each point place one piece of evidence, such as an example or a fact. Common mistake: announcing a topic instead of making a claim. If your thesis could not be argued against, it is probably only a topic.$e$),
(18, $e$Worked example: "Daily reading builds vocabulary. For example, a student who reads fifteen minutes each day meets many new words in the context of a story. Each time the word appears again, its meaning becomes clearer. As a result, the student begins to use those words in writing and speech. Therefore, reading is one of the simplest ways to strengthen language." The first sentence is the topic sentence, the next two give evidence and reasoning, and the last closes the idea. Common mistake: listing facts without explaining how they prove the point.$e$),
(19, $e$Worked example: before revising, "Reading is good and helps people a lot." After revising, "Daily reading expands a student's vocabulary and sharpens focus." The second sentence is specific, uses stronger words, and says exactly how reading helps. When you revise, ask four questions. Is my main idea clear? Is my evidence enough? Is the order logical? Do my words say exactly what I mean? Common mistake: fixing only commas. Revise ideas and organization first, then edit.$e$),
(20, $e$Worked example: before editing, "the students reads every day, and there grades improve." After editing, "The students read every day, and their grades improve." Four errors were fixed: a capital letter, subject and verb agreement, the pair there and their, and punctuation. Read your work aloud, then slowly backward one sentence at a time, to catch errors your eyes skip. Common mistake: trusting spell check alone, since it cannot tell there from their. Use the checklist and a second reader.$e$),
(21, $e$Worked example: in "The arid desert receives almost no rain," the context tells us arid means dry. In "Unlike his generous brother, he was stingy," the signal word unlike points to an antonym clue, so stingy is the opposite of generous. In "She wore garments, such as robes and cloaks," the examples define garments. Common mistake: guessing without proof. Point to the clue words in the sentence, and then check your guess in a dictionary.$e$),
(22, $e$Worked example: the word transgression breaks into trans, meaning across, gress, meaning step, and ion, meaning the act of. Together it means the act of stepping across a line, which fits the Bible's use for breaking God's law. The word benediction has bene, meaning good or well, and dict, meaning to say, so it means a good saying, or blessing. Common mistake: assuming every word is built from parts. Test your guess against the sentence it appears in.$e$),
(23, $e$Worked example: the words frugal, thrifty and cheap all describe someone careful with money. Frugal and thrifty feel positive, while cheap can feel negative. Compare "My uncle is frugal" with "My uncle is cheap." The facts are alike, but the feeling is different. Academic words such as analyze, evidence and significant appear across subjects, so use them correctly: "I will analyze the evidence." Common mistake: using a big word without knowing its meaning. Choose words that fit your meaning and tone.$e$),
(24, $e$Worked example: a semicolon joins two related independent clauses: "Paul wrote many letters; they became part of Scripture." A colon introduces a list or explanation after a complete sentence: "He packed three things: a Bible, a notebook and a pen." A dependent clause needs an independent clause with it: "Because he prepared, he spoke well." Common mistake: leaving a dependent clause alone, as in "Because he prepared." That is a fragment. Check that every sentence has a subject, a verb and a complete thought.$e$))
update course_items i set content = jsonb_set(i.content,'{body}', to_jsonb(replace(i.content->>'body', 'Your task:', e.txt || E'\n\nYour task:'))) from e, courses c where c.id=i.course_id and c.slug='english-1' and i.kind='topic' and i.position=e.pos and i.content->>'body' not like '%Worked example:%';

-- ENGLISH 2
with e(pos, txt) as (values
(1, $e$Worked example: read the line, "She locked the door and did not look back." The literal meaning is that a woman closed a door and walked away. A deeper layer is that she is ending a chapter of her life, and the lock suggests she does not plan to return. Notice that the deeper layer grows out of two details, the lock and the refusal to look back. Common mistake: inventing a deeper meaning with no detail behind it. Every layer you claim must point to words in the text.$e$),
(2, $e$Worked example: suppose a story begins with a boy who hides his mistake and ends with a boy who confesses it and finds peace. The theme could read, "Honesty brings freedom that hiding never can." The theme develops at the moment the boy feels guilty and again at the moment he tells the truth. Common mistake: writing a moral that is a command, such as "Always tell the truth." A theme is a statement about life that the details prove, and it works best when the story shows it rather than preaches it.$e$),
(3, $e$Worked example: a daughter wants to leave home for school far away, while her father fears for her safety. Her perspective is that growth requires risk. His perspective is that love means protecting her. Neither view is simply wrong, and a skilled author lets us feel both. The coming of age moment arrives when the daughter understands her father's fear without giving up her goal. Common mistake: choosing a villain. In conflicting perspectives, ask why each person sees the situation as he or she does.$e$),
(4, $e$Worked example: in a line such as "the light that would not leave the hill," the light could mean the setting sun that lingers, or a memory that lingers in the speaker's mind. Both readings are reasonable, which is ambiguity. A strong answer gives both meanings, then picks one and defends it with another detail from the poem. Common mistake: saying "it can mean anything." Ambiguity allows a small set of meanings that the words support, not every idea a reader might wish.$e$),
(5, $e$Worked example: "Her laughter was a bell" is a metaphor, because it says laughter is a bell. "Her laughter was like a bell" is a simile. Personification gives human action to a nonhuman thing, as in Psalm 98:8, "Let the floods clap their hands." Hyperbole is deliberate exaggeration, such as "I have told you a million times." Common mistake: naming the type and stopping. After the label, explain the feeling or picture the figure creates.$e$),
(6, $e$Worked example: compare "The old house sat on the hill" with "The old house crouched on the hill, its windows dark." The second sentence uses the verb crouched and the detail of dark windows to create a tense, uneasy mood. The tone of the author may be serious or even warning. Mood is what the reader feels. Tone is the author's attitude. Common mistake: using one word such as "sad" for both. Name the mood, then name the words that build it.$e$),
(7, $e$Worked example: original, "The committee postponed the vote because several members were absent." Paraphrase, "The group delayed its decision since many of its members could not attend." Notice that the sentence pattern changed, not only the words. A weak paraphrase swaps a few synonyms and keeps the same order. Common mistake: forgetting the meaning while changing the words. Read your paraphrase beside the original and check that every idea survived.$e$),
(8, $e$Worked example: a modern story about a neighbor who stops to help a stranger on the roadside alludes to the parable of the Good Samaritan in Luke 10. The retelling keeps the helper who crosses a social line, and changes the setting from an ancient road to a city street. An author changes details to reach a new audience while keeping the core message. Common mistake: summarizing the retelling without comparing. Always name what stayed the same and what changed, then say why.$e$),
(9, $e$Worked example: a passage that says "Because the river flooded, the fields were ruined, and as a result the town faced hunger" uses cause and effect. The signal words because and as a result give it away. A heading such as "Flood and Famine" tells the reader what to expect. A timeline would be a helpful added feature if events are given in order. Common mistake: choosing a structure by guess. Quote the signal words that support your choice.$e$),
(10, $e$Worked example: ask what the speaker most wants the audience to do. If a speaker repeats the words "we must" and ends by calling the audience to action, the central idea is a call to responsibility. Repeated words are clues. Supporting details are the examples and reasons that build toward that idea. Common mistake: picking one memorable line and ignoring the rest. The central idea must be large enough to include the key details and the conclusion.$e$),
(11, $e$Worked example: when Lincoln repeats a pattern, such as the balanced phrases of a closing appeal, he uses parallel structure to make his words memorable and solemn. If his purpose is to heal a divided nation, the pattern creates unity in the sound of the sentence as well as in its meaning. Common mistake: naming a device without connecting it to purpose. Use this frame: the author chose ___, which helps the purpose of ___ by ___.$e$),
(12, $e$Worked example: break a short argument into parts. Claim: our town should plant more trees. Reason: trees give shade and clean the air. Evidence: a tree shades a sidewalk and lowers the heat beneath it. Conclusion: therefore, planting trees serves everyone. Now ask whether the reasoning holds. Each reason connects to the claim, so the argument is sound. Common mistake: judging an argument by whether you agree. Judge it by whether the reasons and evidence support the claim.$e$),
(13, $e$Worked example: claim, "Our school should start a reading hour." Reason, "Reading builds focus." Evidence, "Students who read a chapter each day report finishing assignments with fewer breaks." Is it relevant? Yes, because it ties reading to focus. Is it sufficient? Not yet, since one report does not prove the claim. You would want a study or a larger survey. Common mistake: accepting a single example as enough. Ask what more you would need to be convinced.$e$),
(14, $e$Worked example: "As a nurse with twenty years of experience, I can tell you this medicine is safe" is ethos, since it rests on credibility. "Nine out of ten patients improved within a week" is logos, since it uses numbers. "Imagine your child healthy and laughing again" is pathos, since it stirs feeling. Common mistake: thinking that pathos is always bad. Emotion is fair when it is honest and connected to the facts, and it becomes manipulation only when it replaces them.$e$),
(15, $e$Worked example: "Everyone is buying this phone, so it must be the best" is a bandwagon appeal, because popularity does not prove quality. "My cousin ate there once and got sick, so the whole city has bad food" is a hasty generalization, because one case is not enough. A fair rewrite is, "My cousin got sick after eating there, so I will be careful." Common mistake: labeling an argument fallacious because you dislike its conclusion. Name the exact flaw in the reasoning.$e$),
(16, $e$Worked example: "Schools should start later in the morning. Teenagers need more sleep, and rested students learn better. Some argue that a later start would disrupt after school activities. However, schedules can shift together, and the benefit of alert students outweighs the inconvenience. For these reasons, a later start serves the whole community." Notice the claim first, the counterclaim in the middle and a rebuttal that answers it. Common mistake: ignoring the other side. A fair counterclaim makes your argument stronger.$e$),
(17, $e$Worked example: tell, "The dog was scared." Show, "The dog pressed against the wall, trembling, his ears flat." The showing version lets the reader draw the conclusion. Good narrative moves through a problem, rising action, a climax and a resolution. Dialogue and sensory details, such as the smell of rain or the scrape of a chair, make a scene feel real. Common mistake: starting with background and no action. Begin close to the moment when the story begins to move.$e$),
(18, $e$Worked example: topic sentence, "Honeybees keep a hive alive through careful teamwork." Supporting details might be that workers gather nectar, nurses tend the young, and guards protect the entrance. The pattern is a main idea with supporting details. A concluding sentence ties back, "Each bee's small task protects the whole hive." Common mistake: sharing opinions in informative writing. Stay accurate, define key terms and let facts do the teaching.$e$),
(19, $e$Worked example: first draft, "The test was hard. I studied. I still did well." Revision combines the ideas, "Although the test was hard, my careful studying helped me do well." That is a revision because it improves organization and word choice. Editing then checks conventions, such as commas and capital letters. Common mistake: editing too early. If you polish a sentence you later delete, the effort is wasted, so revise first.$e$),
(20, $e$Worked example: a comma splice joins two independent clauses with only a comma. Fix it with a period, a semicolon, or a comma and a conjunction: "The test was hard, so I studied." A complex sentence reads, "Because I was tired, I finished my homework early." A colon introduces a list or an explanation after a complete sentence: "She packed three things: a Bible, a notebook and a pencil." Common mistake: overusing one sentence type. Vary sentence length to give your writing rhythm.$e$),
(21, $e$Worked example: "What about the ocean?" is too broad. A better question is, "How does coral bleaching affect fish populations in the Florida Keys?" It is focused and researchable. A soldier's letter written during the war is a primary source, and a textbook chapter written decades later is a secondary source. When evaluating a website, ask who wrote it, whether it is accurate, and when it was updated. Common mistake: trusting the first search result. Compare at least two reliable sources.$e$),
(22, $e$Worked example: original, "Coral reefs shelter about a quarter of all ocean species." Plagiarism copies these words with no credit. A proper paraphrase reads, "Many ocean species depend on coral reefs for shelter (Lopez 14)." A works cited entry lists the author, title, publisher and year in a set order. Common mistake: believing that changing a few words removes the need for credit. If the idea is not your own, cite it. Honesty is part of good character, as Proverbs 12:22 teaches.$e$),
(23, $e$Worked example: an opening such as "Imagine walking into school and finding every locker open" captures attention. Then state your main message in one sentence and give three supporting points, each with a quick example. Practice aloud with a timer, make eye contact, and speak slowly. As a listener, a respectful response begins with something you learned, such as "I appreciated your point about safety. Could you tell me more about your second example?" Common mistake: reading slides word for word.$e$),
(24, $e$Worked example: in "Unlike his generous brother, he was frugal," the word unlike signals a contrast, so frugal means careful with money. The word unpredictable breaks into un, meaning not, predict, meaning to say beforehand, and able, meaning capable of. Together it means not able to be foretold. Instead of "good speech," a more precise phrase might be "moving speech" or "well organized speech." Common mistake: using a vague word because it is easy. Choose the word that says exactly what you mean.$e$))
update course_items i set content = jsonb_set(i.content,'{body}', to_jsonb(replace(i.content->>'body', 'Your task:', e.txt || E'\n\nYour task:'))) from e, courses c where c.id=i.course_id and c.slug='english-2' and i.kind='topic' and i.position=e.pos and i.content->>'body' not like '%Worked example:%';

-- UNITED STATES HISTORY 1
with e(pos, txt) as (values
(1, $e$Worked example: a good cause and effect answer links events in order. Cause: the North and South built different economies, factories in the North and cotton plantations worked by enslaved people in the South. Event: the Dred Scott decision of 1857 said Black Americans could not be citizens, which outraged many in the North. Effect: after Lincoln won in 1860, South Carolina and other states seceded. Common mistake: naming one cause and calling it the whole story. Historians weigh several causes and show how each led to the next.$e$),
(2, $e$Worked example: to explain the Emancipation Proclamation, state what it did and what it did not do. It declared enslaved people free in the states in rebellion as of January 1, 1863, and it allowed Black men to join the Union army. It did not free enslaved people in the border states loyal to the Union. The 13th Amendment later ended slavery everywhere. Common mistake: saying it freed all enslaved people at once. Precise answers name who was affected and where.$e$),
(3, $e$Worked example: match each amendment to one job. The 13th Amendment ended slavery. The 14th Amendment defined citizenship and promised equal protection of the laws. The 15th Amendment protected the right of male citizens to vote regardless of race. A memory tip is freedom, citizenship, vote. Common mistake: mixing up the order. Make a small chart with the number, the year and the purpose, and review it until you can say it without looking.$e$),
(4, $e$Worked example: after the Compromise of 1877, federal troops left the South, and Southern governments soon passed laws to separate the races and limit Black voting. Poll taxes and literacy tests were tools that took away the vote. Plessy v. Ferguson (1896) approved separate but equal facilities, yet the facilities were never equal. Common mistake: treating the decision as a minor ruling. It gave legal cover to segregation for nearly sixty years, until Brown v. Board of Education in 1954.$e$),
(5, $e$Worked example: to weigh a historical figure, build a two column chart. Under captain of industry, list that Andrew Carnegie built steel mills, lowered prices and later gave away much of his fortune to libraries. Under robber baron, list the low wages and long hours in some industries and the use of power to crush competitors. Then write your judgment with evidence. Common mistake: choosing one label with no evidence. History rewards balanced weighing of facts.$e$),
(6, $e$Worked example: compare two groups with a short frame: "Both ___, but ___." Both the Knights of Labor and the American Federation of Labor wanted better conditions for workers. The Knights welcomed nearly all workers, skilled and unskilled, while the AFL, led by Samuel Gompers, organized skilled workers by trade and focused on wages, hours and conditions. Common mistake: describing each group separately and never comparing. Use signal words such as both, however and in contrast.$e$),
(7, $e$Worked example: push factors drive people out of their homeland, such as famine, poverty and persecution. Pull factors draw them to a new place, such as jobs, land and freedom to worship. An Italian farmer facing hunger was pushed by poverty and pulled by factory work in New York. Ellis Island in New York processed most arrivals from Europe, while Angel Island in California held many Asian immigrants for long, harsh questioning. Common mistake: listing facts without sorting them into push and pull.$e$),
(8, $e$Worked example: tell the story of the Homestead Act in three steps. Offer: 160 acres of public land. Requirement: live on it and farm it for five years. Result: many families, including freed people, built farms on the plains. Then tell the cost: the same land had been home to Native nations, who lost hunting grounds and were pushed onto reservations. Common mistake: telling only one side. A strong answer shows who gained, who lost and why.$e$),
(9, $e$Worked example: write a muckraker entry in one line, "Upton Sinclair wrote The Jungle in 1906, which exposed unsafe meatpacking, and Congress responded with the Pure Food and Drug Act and the Meat Inspection Act." Notice that the entry names the person, the work, the problem and the result. Common mistake: stopping at the exposure. The key idea of the Progressive Era is that public attention led to new laws.$e$),
(10, $e$Worked example: contrast Booker T. Washington and W. E. B. Du Bois with the frame "Washington believed ___, while Du Bois believed ___." Washington urged Black Americans to build skills and economic strength first. Du Bois demanded full civil and political rights at once and helped found the NAACP in 1909. Common mistake: deciding that one man was right and the other wrong. Historians explain each position in its setting before they judge it.$e$),
(11, $e$Worked example: sort the reasons for imperialism into three groups. Economic: markets and resources. Strategic: naval bases, as Mahan urged. Ideological: a belief in spreading American ideas and religion. The Spanish American War of 1898 began after the USS Maine exploded in Havana harbor and ended with the United States gaining Puerto Rico, Guam and the Philippines. Common mistake: copying a single motive. Real events usually have mixed motives that you can name and rank.$e$),
(12, $e$Worked example: use MAIN to remember causes. Militarism: large armies and arms races. Alliances: tangled promises that pulled nations in. Imperialism: rivalry over colonies. Nationalism: fierce pride and rivalry. The assassination in Sarajevo was the spark, not the only cause. America entered in 1917 after German submarine attacks and the Zimmermann Telegram. Common mistake: calling the spark the cause. Separate the long term causes from the event that set them off.$e$),
(13, $e$Worked example: describe a change in daily life with a before and after. Before the 1920s, many families traveled by train or horse and learned the news from papers. Afterward, the Model T and the radio let families travel and hear music and news at home. Prohibition banned alcohol, but illegal sales and organized crime grew, and the 21st Amendment ended it in 1933. Common mistake: listing items with no effect. Always add what changed because of the invention or law.$e$),
(14, $e$Worked example: link causes with the word because. The crash of 1929 happened because stock prices had risen beyond real value, many people had bought on margin with borrowed money, and panic selling began. Banks failed because depositors lost money and demanded cash. Millions lost jobs, and families stood in bread lines. Common mistake: blaming the crash alone for the Depression. Banking failures, falling demand and weak farm prices deepened it.$e$),
(15, $e$Worked example: define each goal and give one example. Relief helped people in need right away, such as jobs through the Civilian Conservation Corps. Recovery rebuilt the economy, such as the Tennessee Valley Authority. Reform prevented future crises, such as Social Security and the FDIC, which insures bank deposits. Praise: it gave hope and jobs. Criticism: critics said it grew government and did not end unemployment until the war. Common mistake: giving only praise. Fair history includes both views.$e$),
(16, $e$Worked example: explain appeasement in three parts. Meaning: giving way to a dictator's demands to keep peace. Example: at Munich in 1938, Britain and France allowed Germany to take part of Czechoslovakia. Result: Hitler was not satisfied, and invaded Poland in 1939. Common mistake: judging only by the outcome. Explain why leaders hoped it would work, that they feared another terrible war, and then why it failed.$e$),
(17, $e$Worked example: build a short timeline. December 7, 1941: Japan attacks Pearl Harbor. December 8: Congress declares war on Japan. December 11: Germany and Italy declare war on the United States. June 1942: the Battle of Midway destroys four Japanese aircraft carriers and turns the Pacific war. Common mistake: mixing the order of events. A timeline with dates helps you explain what led to what.$e$),
(18, $e$Worked example: describe D Day with who, what, where, when and why. On June 6, 1944, Allied forces landed on the beaches of Normandy, France, to open a western front against Germany. Island hopping meant capturing selected Pacific islands to build bases while bypassing others. Navajo code talkers sent messages in a code the enemy never broke. Common mistake: giving a date with no meaning. Always say why the event mattered.$e$),
(19, $e$Worked example: connect rights to wrongs fairly. Americans supported the war with ration books, victory gardens and war bonds. Yet Executive Order 9066 in 1942 forced about 120,000 people of Japanese ancestry, most of them American citizens, into camps without trial, and Congress later apologized and paid reparations in 1988. The Double V campaign called for victory over enemies abroad and over racism at home. Common mistake: excusing injustice as normal for wartime. Name it plainly and explain why it was wrong.$e$),
(20, $e$Worked example: write about the Holocaust with care and precision. Use accurate numbers, about six million Jews, and name the victims fairly. Christians such as Corrie ten Boom hid Jewish neighbors, and Dietrich Bonhoeffer opposed the Nazis and paid with his life. Use respectful language and avoid casual comparisons. Then present both sides of the atomic bomb debate: it may have shortened the war and saved lives, while others argue it caused great suffering. Common mistake: using only opinion. Support each side with reasons.$e$),
(21, $e$Worked example: define containment as the policy of stopping the spread of communism without direct war. The Truman Doctrine sent aid to Greece and Turkey in 1947. The Marshall Plan sent billions of dollars to rebuild Western Europe. In the Cuban Missile Crisis of 1962, Kennedy blockaded Cuba and negotiated, and the missiles were removed. Common mistake: thinking the Cold War had no fighting. It was fought through proxy wars such as Korea and Vietnam.$e$),
(22, $e$Worked example: write the significance of Brown v. Board of Education in one clear sentence: "In 1954 the Supreme Court ruled that separate public schools for Black and white children were unequal, which struck down the legal basis for school segregation." Then connect two laws: the Civil Rights Act of 1964 banned discrimination in public places and employment, and the Voting Rights Act of 1965 protected the right to vote. Common mistake: ignoring the role of ordinary people, such as the church members who organized the Montgomery boycott.$e$),
(23, $e$Worked example: explain why Vietnam divided Americans using two views. Supporters said the war stopped the spread of communism. Opponents said the cost in lives was too great and the goal unclear. Watergate began with a break in at the Democratic headquarters in 1972, and when tapes showed that President Nixon had tried to cover it up, he resigned in 1974. It shows that no one, even the president, is above the law. Common mistake: letting your own opinion replace the evidence.$e$),
(24, $e$Worked example: write about a modern challenge with three steps. Name it, such as division in public life. Explain its cause in a sentence. Then offer a Christian response grounded in Scripture, such as Romans 12:18, "If it be possible, as much as lieth in you, live peaceably with all men." Cold War endings include the fall of the Berlin Wall in 1989 and the collapse of the Soviet Union in 1991. Common mistake: ending with a slogan. A strong answer offers a specific action a citizen can take.$e$))
update course_items i set content = jsonb_set(i.content,'{body}', to_jsonb(replace(i.content->>'body', 'Your task:', e.txt || E'\n\nYour task:'))) from e, courses c where c.id=i.course_id and c.slug='united-states-history-1' and i.kind='topic' and i.position=e.pos and i.content->>'body' not like '%Worked example:%';
